<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology | Vira Trend Reports"
  description="How Vira collects TikTok trend data and computes growth, trust and competition for its public trend reports."
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <p>
    Data comes from TikTok Creative Center's Creative Search Insights, collected by a single
    Vira-owned TikTok account. It is not TikTok's own public data export and is not affiliated
    with or endorsed by TikTok.
  </p>

  <h2>Growth</h2>
  <p>
    Growth is a multiplier: the current search-demand level for a topic divided by its level at
    the start of a 7&ndash;21 day window (<code>level_now / level_base</code>). A report only
    shows a growth number when Vira's own <strong>trust</strong> signal for that number is
    medium or high &mdash; low- and no-trust topics are left out of the ranking rather than shown
    with a misleading number.
  </p>

  <h2>Trust levels</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>high</strong> &mdash; a clean, topic-specific curve with a full growth window.</li>
    <li><strong>med</strong> &mdash; usable, but the value is quantized, lagged, or the window is
      only partially filled.</li>
    <li><strong>low</strong> &mdash; based on a shared/generic curve, a coarse scale, a spike, or
      a missing window; not used for the ranking.</li>
    <li><strong>none</strong> &mdash; no usable time series yet; not published.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Competition (video count)</h2>
  <p>
    "Videos (reported)" is the video count TikTok itself reports for a topic. TikTok returns
    <code>0</code> for some topics rather than an actual count &mdash; Vira shows this as
    "not reported" instead of "0" so it isn't read as zero competition.
  </p>

  <h2>Why numbers aren't shown as an absolute search volume</h2>
  <p>
    TikTok occasionally reports the identical demand level for multiple, unrelated topics
    (rounding/bucketing on TikTok's side, most visible on LLM-generated topic names). Reports on
    this site avoid publishing that raw level as if it were an exact search volume, and prefer
    topics without this bucketing for any example with a specific number attached.
  </p>

  <h2>Update frequency (TikTok)</h2>
  <p>TikTok reports are recomputed daily from the latest data pulled by Vira's collector.</p>

  <h2>YouTube reports</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube reports (e.g. <a href="/reports/youtube/food">Food channel growth</a>) use
    <code>vira_analytics.channels_latest_mat</code>, a daily snapshot of public YouTube channel
    statistics (subscribers, views) collected by Vira's own channel-tracking pipeline &mdash; not
    TikTok Creative Center data. <strong>Growth %</strong> is subscribers gained in the last 30
    days divided by the subscriber count 30 days earlier, shown only for channels with a complete
    30-day history and at least 50,000 current subscribers, to exclude brand-new channels with no
    real baseline. <strong>Topic</strong> is YouTube's own classification for the channel; channels
    tagged with more than two topics are excluded to keep the niche match tight. Currently a manual
    snapshot rather than a daily pipeline &mdash; see the report page for the data-as-of date.
  </p>

  <h2>More</h2>
  <p>
    Full TikTok and YouTube analytics, trend discovery beyond these public reports, and an MCP
    server for AI agents are at <a href="https://tryvira.app">tryvira.app</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
